   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to requiring the department\nof health of the state of New York to conduct a study of the effects of racial\nand ethnic disparities on infant mortality and prepare and submit a report to\nthe governor and the legislature",
    "basePrintNo": "S6662",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6662-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 63,
       "fullName": "Timothy M. Kennedy",
       "imgName": "429_timothy_m._kennedy.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 429,
       "sessionMemberId": 1205,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "KENNEDY"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §2500-l, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6662\n\nSPONSOR: BENJAMIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public health law, in relation to\nrequiring the department of health of the state of New York to conduct a\nstudy of the effects of racial and ethnic disparities on infant mortal-\nity and prepare and submit a report to the governor and the legislature\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill establishes a department of persons specializing in the fields\nof neonatal and post neonatal pathology, maternal and infant health,\nminority health advocacy, or other related field. Within a year after\nthe effective date of this section, the department shall prepare and\nsubmit a report containing the study's findings concerning the effects\nof racial and ethnic disparities on infant mortality and recommendations\nfor legislative or other actions that can be undertaken to reduce infant\nmortality and improve birth outcomes in the states, reduce or eliminate\nracial and ethnic disparities that contribute to infant mortality and to\nincrease access to health care services among racial and ethnic popu-\nlations in the state.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThe public health law is amended by adding a new section 2500-1 to\nconduct a study on the effects of racial and ethnic disparities on\ninfant mortality. The purpose of the study shall be to identify state-\nwide and regional infant death rates, trends, patterns, and racial and\nethnic disparities in adverse birth outcomes, and medical, non-medical,\nand system-related factors that may have contributed to infant deaths\nand treatment disparities. In addition, the study reviews the extent to\nwhich racial and ethnic disparities in infant mortality in a geographic\narea indicate the need to increase access to health care services among\nracial and ethnic populations in that area. Lastly, the study plans to\nidentify ways to reduce or eliminate racial and ethnic disparities that\ncontribute to infant mortality.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2018, New York State had 4.5 infant deaths rate per 1,000 live\nbirths. Albeit a decrease since 2016, New York State's infant death rate\nis still one of the highest in the country. With black woman effected\nmore than any other racial or social group, this bill requires the\ndepartment of health of the state of New York to conduct a study to\ndetermine the effects of racial and ethnic disparities on infant mortal-\nity and prepare and submit a report to the governor and the legislature.\nThe study will provide legislatures with external factors affecting\nstatewide and regional infant death rates, trends, patterns, and racial\nand ethnic disparities in adverse birth outcomes, and medical, non-medi-\ncal, and system-related factors that may have contributed to infant\ndeaths and treatment disparities.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone\n\n \nLOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThirtieth day after becoming law.\n",
